For a reversible isothermal expansion, the work is

\[
w_{\rm rev}=-\int_{V_1}^{V_2} P\,dV
\]

(using the chemistry sign convention: expansion work is negative).

For a gas obeying the Peng–Robinson equation of state,

\[
P=\frac{RT}{V-b}-\frac{a\alpha}{V^2+2bV-b^2}
\]

where \(a\), \(b\), and \(\alpha\) are the Peng–Robinson parameters. In an isothermal process, \(T\) is constant, so \(a\alpha\) is constant as well.

So,

\[
w_{\rm rev}
=-\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\left[\frac{RT}{V-b}-\frac{a\alpha}{V^2+2bV-b^2}\right]dV
\]

Split the integral:

\[
w_{\rm rev}
=-RT\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\frac{dV}{V-b}
+a\alpha\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\frac{dV}{V^2+2bV-b^2}
\]

The first integral is straightforward:

\[
\int \frac{dV}{V-b}=\ln(V-b)
\]

For the second, factor the quadratic:

\[
V^2+2bV-b^2=(V+b)^2-2b^2
\]

Let \(u=V+b\), then

\[
\int \frac{dV}{V^2+2bV-b^2}
=\int \frac{du}{u^2-(\sqrt2 b)^2}
=\frac{1}{2\sqrt2\,b}\ln\left|\frac{u-\sqrt2 b}{u+\sqrt2 b}\right|
\]

Thus,

\[
\int \frac{dV}{V^2+2bV-b^2}
=\frac{1}{2\sqrt2\,b}
\ln\left|\frac{V+b-\sqrt2 b}{V+b+\sqrt2 b}\right|
\]

Now apply limits \(V_1\to V_2\):

\[
w_{\rm rev}
=-RT\ln\frac{V_2-b}{V_1-b}
+\frac{a\alpha}{2\sqrt2\,b}
\ln\left[
\frac{V_2+b-\sqrt2 b}{V_2+b+\sqrt2 b}
\cdot
\frac{V_1+b+\sqrt2 b}{V_1+b-\sqrt2 b}
\right]
\]

This can be written more compactly as

\[
w_{\rm rev}
=-RT\ln\frac{V_2-b}{V_1-b}
+\frac{a\alpha}{2\sqrt2\,b}
\ln\left[
\frac{(V_2+(1-\sqrt2)b)(V_1+(1+\sqrt2)b)}
{(V_2+(1+\sqrt2)b)(V_1+(1-\sqrt2)b)}
\right]
\]

If one uses the physics sign convention (“work done by the gas”), the result is the negative of this expression.

