\[
\phi=\frac{\text{number of molecules decomposed}}{\text{number of photons absorbed}}
\]

For the reaction \(2\text{HI} \to \text{H}_2+\text{I}_2\), the statement “quantum yield = 1.99” means about 1.99 molecules of HI are decomposed per photon absorbed.

First find the energy of one photon of wavelength \(254\,\text{nm}\):

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{hc}{\lambda}
\]

Using \(h=6.626\times 10^{-34}\,\text{J s}\), \(c=3.0\times 10^8\,\text{m s}^{-1}\), \(\lambda=254\times 10^{-9}\,\text{m}\),

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{(6.626\times 10^{-34})(3.0\times 10^8)}{254\times 10^{-9}}
\approx 7.82\times 10^{-19}\,\text{J}
\]

Now calculate the number of photons absorbed from 3070 J:

\[
N_{\text{photons}}=\frac{3070}{7.82\times 10^{-19}}
\approx 3.93\times 10^{21}
\]

With quantum yield \(1.99\), the number of HI molecules decomposed is

\[
N_{\text{HI}}=1.99\times 3.93\times 10^{21}
\approx 7.82\times 10^{21}\ \text{molecules}
\]

Convert to moles:

\[
n=\frac{7.82\times 10^{21}}{6.022\times 10^{23}}
\approx 1.30\times 10^{-2}\ \text{mol}
\]

So the moles of HI decomposed are about \(1.3\times 10^{-2}\) mol.

