A cocktail remnant is a complex mixture: mostly water and ethanol at first, plus dissolved sugars, organic acids, salts, flavor compounds, and perhaps fruit juice residues. After several days, the volatile components evaporate. Ethanol leaves quickly, water more slowly, so the remaining liquid becomes progressively enriched in the nonvolatile solutes.

That immediately suggests crystallization and/or formation of a dried amorphous film. If the concentration of sugars or salts exceeds solubility during evaporation, nucleation and crystal growth can occur on the glass bottom. Crystals often grow in irregular patches, and as neighboring crystals impinge they can create grain boundaries and a visibly fractured or mosaic-like texture.

But many cocktail residues are rich in sugar and other organics that do not always form large clean crystals; instead they can dry into a glassy or polymer-like film. Fruit pulp, syrups, proteins, or colloidal material can produce a thin solid film adhered to the glass. As drying continues, that film loses solvent and shrinks. Because it sticks to the glass, the shrinkage is constrained, so tensile stress builds in the plane of the film.

When stress exceeds the mechanical strength of the film, it relieves itself by cracking. This is the same general mechanism as mud cracks or drying paint cracks. The resulting pattern can look like a network of polygons or fractures. Repeated humidity cycles over several days can intensify this: slight re-dissolution and re-drying may reorganize the residue, sharpen crystal edges, and propagate cracks.

So the fractured pattern likely comes from a combination:
1) evaporation concentrates dissolved material;
2) some components crystallize, seeding rigid domains;
3) other components form a continuous dried film;
4) differential shrinkage between crystalline regions, amorphous residue, and the glass substrate generates stress;
5) the brittle residue fractures into a crack network.

