Wheeler’s delayed choice experiment is a version of the double-slit/interferometer idea in which the choice of how to measure a photon is made only after the photon has already entered the apparatus. In a typical setup, a photon passes through a first beam splitter and is put into a superposition of two paths. If a second beam splitter is present, the two paths recombine and produce interference, showing wave-like behavior. If the second beam splitter is absent, the paths remain separate and the detectors reveal which path was taken, showing particle-like behavior.

The key feature is that the decision to insert or remove the second beam splitter is delayed until after the photon has passed the first beam splitter. Even so, the result matches the later measurement choice: interference appears only when the setup allows the paths to recombine, while definite path detection appears when which-path information is available.

This demonstrates wave-particle duality because the same quantum object can display either wave-like or particle-like properties depending on the measurement arrangement. The experiment suggests that subatomic particles do not carry fixed classical properties independent of observation. Instead, quantum behavior is described by a superposition, and measurement determines which aspect becomes observable.

Its main implications are:
- quantum particles are not simply classical waves or classical particles;
- interference and which-path information are complementary and cannot both be fully observed at once;
- measurement plays an active role in determining the observed outcome;
- the behavior of a quantum system cannot be understood as having a definite pre-existing classical history independent of the experimental setup.

Thus, Wheeler’s delayed choice experiment reinforces the quantum idea that what is observed depends fundamentally on how the system is measured.

