A shock wave is fundamentally a pressure disturbance that propagates through matter faster than the local sound speed. So the first thing to note is that “an explosion in space” behaves very differently depending on whether there is any surrounding material.

If the explosion occurs in near-vacuum, there is no dense atmosphere to compress. That means there is no Earth-like blast wave with a strong pressure front traveling long distances through air. Instead, the hot debris and radiation from the explosion expand outward freely. The ejecta themselves can move at high speed, and photons (light, X-rays, gamma rays) and electromagnetic fields carry a large fraction of the energy away.

A true shock wave in space can still form if there is some medium at all—interplanetary gas, plasma, dust, or the interstellar medium. Even though that medium is very rarefied, a rapidly expanding shell from the explosion can sweep it up and drive a shock into it. In space this is often better thought of as a plasma shock, influenced by electromagnetic forces as well as ordinary gas pressure. Because the density is so low, the shock is weaker in terms of overpressure at a given distance than in air, and it develops over much larger distances and timescales.

The absence of a dense atmosphere affects the dynamics in several ways:
- little or no strong acoustic blast in the usual sense;
- most energy goes into radiation, fast particles, and kinetic energy of ejecta rather than compressing nearby gas;
- expansion is closer to free expansion initially, because there is little resistance;
- heating and damage come more from radiation and direct impact of ejecta/plasma than from a pressure wave.

This is similar to supernovae in the sense that a supernova also ejects matter and radiation into a very low-density medium, and the outward-moving ejecta drive a shock into surrounding interstellar gas. The difference is mainly scale and source: supernovae are vastly more energetic, often relativistic in parts, and produce long-lived expanding shock fronts (supernova remnants). The underlying physics—fast ejecta plus radiation expanding into tenuous plasma and forming shocks only when interacting with ambient matter—is qualitatively similar.

