So the minimum speed is about \(6.8\times 10^3\ \text{m s}^{-1}\), assuming all of the F atom’s kinetic energy is available to dissociate \( \mathrm{D_2} \).

Use the de Broglie relation:
\[
\lambda=\frac{h}{p}
\]
For a non-relativistic electron, momentum can be found from kinetic energy:
\[
K.E.=\frac{p^2}{2m}\quad \Rightarrow \quad p=\sqrt{2mK}
\]
Given:
\[
m=9.1\times 10^{-31}\,\text{kg},\qquad K=3.0\times 10^{-25}\,\text{J}
\]
So,
\[
p=\sqrt{2(9.1\times 10^{-31})(3.0\times 10^{-25})}
\]
\[
p=\sqrt{54.6\times 10^{-56}}
=\sqrt{5.46\times 10^{-55}}
\approx 7.39\times 10^{-28}\,\text{kg m s}^{-1}
\]
Now using Planck’s constant \(h=6.63\times 10^{-34}\,\text{J s}\),
\[
\lambda=\frac{6.63\times 10^{-34}}{7.39\times 10^{-28}}
\approx 8.97\times 10^{-7}\,\text{m}
\]
Thus the wavelength is about
\[
9.0\times 10^{-7}\,\text{m}
\]
which is \(0.9\,\mu\text{m}\).

A quick check: the kinetic energy is very small, so a sub-micrometer wavelength is reasonable for an electron.

